# Functions passed to functions, with nothing written down.
#
# An unannotated function passed as an argument is not guessed at: it
# travels as the incomplete thing it is, and is worked out where it is
# finally *called*, inside the body that received it. So `add_one` below
# is checked when `f(f(x))` calls it -- not when it is handed over.
#
# `apply_twice` itself is worked out once, at its first call, and what
# that call teaches is separated into the part its body depended on and
# the part it did not. Its body never asks what `x` is: it only hands it
# to `f` and hands the answer back. So nothing about it is Int, and its
# type says so with a variable, which is what lets the second call pass
# strings through the very same definition.

def apply_twice(f, x):
    f(f(x))

def add_one(n):
    n + 1

def repeat(word):
    word ++ word

print(apply_twice(add_one, 5))
print(apply_twice(repeat, 'ab'))

# A parameter whose function type *is* written down is a claim about
# what may be passed, and an unannotated function may still satisfy it:
# it is worked out at the types the annotation names, right there at the
# call, since the body receiving it is checked once and will not reach
# it again.
def apply_once(f:(Int) -> Int, x:Int) -> Int:
    f(x)

print(apply_once(add_one, 10))

# Binding it is how the whole type gets printed.
applied = apply_twice
